written("w1_improve_timing_experiment", 1, 5, "1e",
        stem="A student measures the period of a pendulum by timing one oscillation with a stopwatch and gets 1.3 s, then 1.6 s, then 1.1 s. Evaluate this method and describe a better procedure, explaining why it is better.",
        points=[P("timing one oscillation is unreliable because human reaction time (about 0.2 s) is a large fraction of such a short time, which explains the scatter", ["reaction time", "0.2 s", "large fraction", "too short", "scatter", "varies", "inconsistent", "unreliable"]),
                P("time a large number of oscillations (e.g. 20) and divide by that number", ["20 oscillations", "many oscillations", "large number", "divide by", "several oscillations"]),
                P("this spreads the reaction-time error over many oscillations so its effect on each period is much smaller; start and stop at the rest position and repeat to average", ["spreads", "smaller effect", "reduced", "divided among", "less significant", "rest position", "repeat", "average"])],
        min_points=2,
        model_answer="Timing a single oscillation is poor because the student's reaction time (about 0.2 s) is a large fraction of a period of roughly 1.3 s, which is why the readings scatter so widely. A better method is to time 20 complete oscillations, starting and stopping as the bob passes the rest position, and divide the total by 20. The reaction-time error is then shared across 20 oscillations, so its effect on each period is 20 times smaller; repeating and averaging improves the result further.",
        hints=["How big is a person's reaction time compared with the time being measured?",
               "If one error of 0.2 s is spread over 20 swings, how much does it affect each period?",
               "Evaluate the method, give the improved procedure, and explain why it is better."],
        keywords="reaction time large vs single period; time 20 oscillations ÷ 20; error shared, repeat",
        figure=lambda: sketches.pendulum_sketch(),
        sample_pass=["Timing one swing is unreliable because reaction time is a big fraction of 1.3 s; time 20 oscillations and divide by 20 so the error is spread out, then repeat",
                     "the scatter comes from reaction time; counting many oscillations makes the error per period much smaller"],
        sample_fail=["Use a longer string so the period is shorter", "The method is fine, just take the middle value"])

